hi i'm stephen wolfram and i'd like to  show you a bit about our Wolfram  language you know I've been working  towards what's now the Wolfram language  for about 30 years and big parts of it  I've been able to incubate in  Mathematica and Wolfram Alpha but it's  only in recent times that we finally had  what we need to create the whole Wolfram  language and to set up mechanisms to  deploy it very widely we're actually at  an incredibly exciting point right now  where we're suddenly able to take  computation to a whole new level and in  effect  inject sophisticated computation into  everything so what is the Wolfram  language well the first thing to say is  that it's a new kind of thing it's what  I call a knowledge-based language it's a  language where a vast amount of  knowledge about how to do computations  and about the world is built right into  the language so right within the  language there are primitives for  processing images or laying out networks  or looking up stock prices or creating  interfaces or solving optimization  problems or whatever and through the  symbolic structure of the language  they're all set up to work together in a  wonderfully coherent way let me show you  one of the great things about the  Wolfram language is that if you want to  you can use it completely interactively  even on the web so you can type  something in and immediately get a  result out like this or like this  yes it's fast of course it knows how to  factor that number two it's got all the  fancy algorithms built right in it's  also got immediate visualization it also  knows right off the bat like how to get  your friend Network from Facebook it  lays out the network automatically and  it knows all the fancy graph theory  algorithms so you can immediately ask it  all kinds of things about the network  like how do your friends break into  groups or what's the most connections  any friend has the Wolfram language  knows how to do lots of things like  let's ask it to get the current image  from my computer's camera  ah that's me well let's like edge detect  me I can have it be dynamic - I can do  all kinds of computations like there's  me broken into little squares and  there's me with a random color blended  into each square and now reassembled the  orphan language doesn't just know about  algorithms and computations it also  knows about the world  like let's ask it when the Sun will set  today there's the answer for my current  location there's the time from sunrise  to sunset in days we can convert it to  minutes  you know the Wolfram language has a  precise way of talking about everything  but when we're dealing with the world at  large we often just want to use natural  language to describe what we're talking  about and thanks to all from alpha we  built a big stack of technology for  doing that like we can just ask for  countries in South America and our  natural language understanding system  can figure out what that means and  generate precise Wolfram language code  from it so that we get a list of the  countries and now we can compute from  that like let's generate their flags or  let's find the dominant colors in those  flags well let's have a whole little  program to make a table of countries in  their dominant flag colors okay let's  try something more ambitious let's get a  list of the capitals of the countries in  Western Europe now let's find what order  we'd have to visit these in to have the  shortest tour of all of them needless to  say the Wolfram language has the best  known algorithms for solving a so called  Traveling Salesman problem like that now  let's plot the tour on a map as a thick  red line and yes the Wolfram language  has detailed maps of everywhere built in  okay well I've been doing different  kinds of examples there's really a huge  amount built into the Wolfram language  you can get a sense of it here thousands  of algorithmic functions thousands of  domains of data and knowledge but in a  sense that's just the beginning because  there's so much more power from being  able to put all these things together  and what makes that possible is the  structure of the Wolfram language and  the principles that it's built on the  first really critical thing to  understand about the Wolfram language is  that it's symbolic if you have X you  don't have to give it a value you can  just have it be symbolic X you can type  in a formula involving X and then you  can do a computation on the formula  symbolically and of course this is great  for math but one of the big discoveries  of the Wolfram language is that it gives  you a way to do a zillion other things  too let's say you've got some data with  a part you don't know  you can just treat it symbolically what  about something like a network well on  the Wolfram language it's a symbolic  expression too that displays as a graph  but you can go ahead and manipulate it  symbolically - same with 3d graphics go  ahead and create a symbolic form render  it as 3d graphics now because everything  is symbolic I can just pick up this  output and say feed it into an image  processing algorithm or I can make a  table of rotated versions of the image  something else that's a symbolic  expression of the world from language is  a user interface element like a slider I  can make a whole grid of them build up  any user interface all just represented  by a symbolic expression same with  documents and document elements - all  just symbolic  and once things are symbolic it's easy  to do meta operations on them like take  a plot and effectively just pick it up  and make a dynamic version of it  you can make a dynamic version of  anything and because everything is a  symbolic expression it all just works  you know any function can be treated  symbolically here's just F symbolic F  and actually underneath the full form of  everything in the language is like this  just a bunch of symbolic functions  representing formulas or datasets or  documents or cities or whatever so what  can you do with these symbolic functions  well one thing is incredibly powerful  functional programming neste functions  symbolically or put more and more frames  around something or make a kind of  nested interface  okay here's something more ambitious  let's use functional programming to do  some web crawling the Wolfram language  already knows how to import lots of  things like hyperlinks from my website  it's a little more code but let's nested  lis crawl out a couple of levels that  make a graph of what that corner of the  web looks like you know you can make  symbolic pure functions to do all sorts  of things like this takes the list of  English words that are built into the  world from language and creates a  function to find which one is nearest to  a given word now you can pick up this  function and apply it to any word and  make a simple spell corrector pure  functions and functional programming get  in everywhere here's selecting  palindrome words based on a simple  symbolic pure function you can  generalize this dramatically and for  example get a shockingly powerful  symbolic way to query databases and  large hierarchical collections of data  in a sense the Wolfram language is kind  of the ultimate fusion language because  it's symbolic nature lets it encompass  pretty much any style of programming one  that's very powerful and kind of  uniquely symbolic is pattern based  programming  the idea is you give a pattern for  transforming some class of symbolic  expressions like this says that anytime  there's F of some particular nested list  structure here's the value so now if we  give F this kind of structure it'll  produce a result but if the pattern  doesn't match it'll just stay symbolic  and this is basically how all function  definitions in the Wolfram language work  the Wolfram language is a huge language  by far the largest ever but it's built  on a small set of powerful principles  the first is coherence the idea that  everything in the language must fit  together I must say that I've spent a  fair fraction of the past 30 years  working to make this happen  designing every corner of the language  so it's as consistent and unified and  integrated as possible so it's easy to  understand and so one can take something  from over here and be confident that  it'll feed right into something over  there  I've had another principle to maximum  automation the idea that the language  should take care of as much as possible  let's say you're doing machine learning  and you want to build a data classifier  well there are all sorts of methods for  doing that but in the world from  language there's just one super function  classify that's packed with meta  algorithms to automatically figure out  what to do of course if you want you can  go in and control every detail but  otherwise you can just treat it like a  black box one of thousands of super  functions and the world from language  that effectively give you the highest  possible level building blocks for  programs and these building blocks don't  just contain algorithms  they've got knowledge and data to  knowledge about how to import and export  hundreds of formats knowledge about how  to interact with all sorts of external  api's knowledge about how to compute  properties of systems in the world and  also huge amounts of curated computable  data stored in the world from cloud and  continuously updated the same data that  powers Wolfram Alpha completely  programmatically accessible but ok so  the Wolfram language makes it really  easy to create really powerful programs  but what can you do with them well it  turns out that the symbolic nature of  the language does something great here  too because it lets the language define  and describe its own deployment let's  say you set up a function in the Wolfram  language you can run it in a notebook  session or you can just take the  symbolic code and deploy it to the cloud  you can just go to the cloud object from  the web and if you do you'll see a web  interface which automatically runs your  Wolfram language code in the cloud and  by the way you can set up a mobile app  to do the same kind of thing the  interface here uses CDF our computable  document format running in the cloud and  in fact you can run the whole native  notebook interface for the Wolfram  language in the cloud here it is you can  use notebooks as templates for reports  that get filled in by running Wolfram  language code you can schedule often  language code to run on cloud servers at  particular time say sending you mail you  can take any Wolfram language function  and set up an instant API for it that  you can call from anywhere on the web  like this asks for a JSON output of some  weather data for the users geolocation  if you have an embedded Wolfram engine  you can get code to embed a call to this  in lots of languages and you can  actually call the code without even  going to the web one thing about the  Wolfram language is that it's completely  scalable you can write tiny one-line  programs that are already very powerful  or you can write multi million line  programs like Wolfram Alpha  it's so much easier to build and debug  symbolic programs because at every stage  every piece is meaningful and depending  on your style of programming there are  various IDE s for the Wolfram language  that take advantage of the symbolic  character of the language you can use  the Wolfram language for  high-performance computing - it's got  parallelism built right into the  language so this is automatically  starting up and running lots of  different processes across my little  heterogeneous network and collecting the  results you can also use the Wolfram  language on the upcoming generation of  tiny embedded processors and for example  it's included with the OS for the  Raspberry Pi Linux computer you can use  the Wolfram language to interact with  devices in fact the Wolfram language is  in a unique position with respect to  devices and with respect to real-world  data in general because in a sense it's  a language that has a model of the world  built in and in fact wdf the Wolfram  data framework encapsulate that model  and within the Wolfram language there  are lots of ways of taking unstructured  data and getting it into wdf with its  units and dates and entities and so on  made canonical when it comes to devices  the Wolfram connected devices project is  curating them to make them automatically  accessible from within the language ok  so I've tried to give some indication of  what the Wolfram language is about so  how can you get started like after every  output there's a suggestions bar that  suggests what you might want to do next  and you can even try just giving input  in natural language and starting to  construct your programs that way and  when you've got a program you like you  can deploy it in all sorts of ways my  goal with the Wolfram language has been  to encapsulate as much computational  knowledge as possible so people can go  from ideas to deployed products as  quickly and easily as possible  I've also been very pleased that the  Wolfram language seems to be uniquely  good as a first programming language  because you can do so much so easily and  quickly get exposed to such a broad  range of methods and topics I've been  working towards the Wolfram language for  30 years we laid strong foundations very  early and we've just kept on building  and actually I keep on being amazed at  what's possible and now we've got a vast  stack of technology and content that I  think just completely changes how we  should think about programming I get a  big kick out of using it every day I  hope you'll have a chance  to try it and that you'll end up doing  amazing things with the well firm  language thanks very much
 

